
Both RestTemplate and WebClient are Spring tools used when your application needs to call
another HTTP API.

For example:

Your Order Service
       |
       | GET /users/10
       ↓
User Service

Your service needs an HTTP client to make that call.

1. RestTemplate

RestTemplate is the older, synchronous/blocking HTTP client in Spring.

Example:

RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();

User user = restTemplate.getForObject(
    "http://user-service/users/10",
    User.class
);

The important part is:
request → wait → response
The current thread waits until the response comes back.

So:

RestTemplate = synchronous + blocking

It is still widely seen in existing Spring applications, but for new development,
Spring generally recommends WebClient.

2. WebClient

WebClient is Spring's modern HTTP client from Spring WebFlux.

Example:

WebClient webClient = WebClient.create();

User user = webClient.get()
        .uri("http://user-service/users/10")
        .retrieve()
        .bodyToMono(User.class)
        .block();

Notice:

.bodyToMono(User.class)

The response is represented as a Mono<User>.

Notice 2:

Without .block():

Mono<User> user = webClient.get()
        .uri("http://user-service/users/10")
        .retrieve()
        .bodyToMono(User.class);

This is non-blocking/reactive.

One important confusion

You might see:
WebClient
    ...
    .block();

Then someone says:

"But WebClient is non-blocking!"

Technically, WebClient itself supports non-blocking communication, but:

.block()

makes your calling code wait for the result.

So:

Mono<User> user = webClient.get()
        ...
        .bodyToMono(User.class);

➡️ reactive/non-blocking style

while:
User user = webClient.get()
        ...
        .bodyToMono(User.class)
        .block();

➡️ you're using WebClient in a blocking manner.

RestTemplate vs WebClient

|                 | RestTemplate                  | WebClient                      |
| --------------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| Style           | Blocking                      | Non-blocking/reactive          |
| API style       | Synchronous                   | Reactive                       |
| Introduced      | Older                         | Modern                         |
| Spring module   | Spring MVC                    | Spring WebFlux                 |
| Return type     | `User`                        | `Mono<User>` / `Flux<User>`    |
| Good for        | Existing/simple blocking apps | Reactive/high-concurrency apps |
| New development | Usually not preferred         | Generally preferred            |

Visual difference

RestTemplate:

Thread
  │
  ├── Send request
  │
  ├── WAIT 💤
  │
  └── Receive response

WebClient:

Thread
  │
  ├── Send request
  │
  ├── Do other work
  │
  └── Process response when available

